  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a combination of multi-start dense search, smooth optimization, and adaptive restarting to escape local minima. This addresses the bottleneck of previous implementations that struggled with periodic patterns and multi-modal optimization. By maintaining multiple restart points across scales, using smooth surrogate functions, and incorporating clear line search strategies, this approach should find better solutions. The key limitations are: it requires tracking multiple populations with strict budget management, and it may need to explicitly handle periodicity constraints in some measurements, but it offers clearer convergence guarantees than previous heuristic methods.\n\n2. Mathspecification\n- Objective: minimize C5 where C5 = max_k \u222bh(x) * (1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (dx=2/n), n = n_points\n- Representation: h is a vector of step function samples\n- Objective evaluation: C5 = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")[k]*(2.0/n)) (only evaluate k with available shifts)\n- Solver: CvXPY with exponential smoothing, Ensures global optimality certificates for small N, \n  For large N: project-constrained smooth optimization using softmax surrogates\n- Any violated constraints trigger repair by: clipping values to [0,1], then redistributing mass with current centroid\n\n3. Implementation procedure    \nInitialize:\n  if 'initial_h_values' in globals(): start from parent\n  Set grid sizes: [n//level for level in range(1,4) if n//level>=10]\n  Track population = {grid_size: [h_vector, best_c]}\n\nFor each grid_size:\n  Sample M=15 seeds (maintain 3/4 transferred from larger grids)\n  For each seed:\n    - Repair to feasible solution (0\u2264h\u22641, \u2211h*dx=1)\n    - Use SLSQP with exponential smoothing of max value\n    - After local optimum, use multi-start hill climbing from random thresholds\n\nGlobal management:\n  Compute urgent budget usage before each iteration, When >80% exhausted: reduce restart budget\n  Maintain worst->best solution comparison history across grids\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Grid levels: [n=180,90,60] (unless time restricts)\n- Iterator options: \n  - Moderate stochastic movement (50% target fraction)\n  - Sample shifts systematically k\u2208(0.1*width,0.9*width)\n- Dataset strategies:\n  - \u03b3=0.0\u21920.5 (accuracy vsESC convergence)\n  - Switch to gradient descent for feature-vector flats\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n  Dominant cost: N-points^2*log(iteration) (lower for finer grids)\n  Toll 800s for 300\u00d7 optimized\u2192 margin optimal; earlier cut if estimates suggest\n  Monitor fit bounds, use restarted ensembles for highest n at lower resolution\n  Fallback: smallest grid (12 points)\u2192 0.4 x\u21a6 test point\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n  Validate:\n    - Floating point checks (not NaN/Inf)\n    - \u2211h*dx \u22431 (tol=1e-5)\n    - Compute C5 exactly for all k present (not masked)\n    - Pack results: [h.tolist(), C5, n_points]\n  Include C5 evaluation as per:\n    corr= np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")\n    C5 = 2.0/n * np.max(corr)\n\n  Bonus: Semidefinite programming formulation for feasibility check\n  But final proof is direct calculation",
